  <script>
/* =========================================================================
   SHEETS_CONFIG
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Optional. Leave everything blank ("") and the site behaves exactly as
   before — Units, Inquiries, and People/Topics/Events all come straight
   from data.js, same as always.

   To pull any of them from a Google Sheet instead:
     1. In Google Sheets, put each one on its own tab (e.g. "Units",
        "Inquiries", "Entities") with a header row matching the columns
        below.
     2. File → Share → Publish to web → pick that tab → CSV → Publish.
     3. Copy the link it gives you and paste it below.

   If a sheet fails to load for ANY reason (wrong link, not published,
   offline, a typo in a column name), the site quietly ignores it and
   falls back to what's already written in data.js. Nothing breaks —
   worst case, that one piece just doesn't update from the sheet.

   Column headers each tab needs (extra columns are ignored):

     Units tab:      id | number | title | era
     Inquiries tab:  id | question
     Entities tab:   id | name | category | role | type | actions | definition | image
     Lessons tab:    id | unit id | number | title
     Timeline tab:   id | date | title | details | category

   The "id" values must match the ids already used in data.js (unit1,
   inq1, e1, etc.) — that's how a sheet row and a lesson's reference to
   it get matched up.

   On the Entities tab, "role" and "type" are two names for the same
   column — use whichever fits the row. For a person, use "role" for
   their specific position (President, Muckraker, Suffragist, etc.).
   For a topic or event, use "type" for its kind (Law, Event, Belief,
   etc.). Leave whichever one doesn't apply blank. It also keeps
   quiz wrong answers sensible — a president's wrong answers are drawn
   from other presidents, not muckrakers, as long as there are enough
   of that specific role to draw from.

   "actions" holds one or more things that person did, separated by
   " ||| " (the same separator readings use for paragraphs), e.g.
   "Founded Standard Oil ||| Broke up in a 1911 antitrust ruling". When a
   person has no written definition, their actions are listed on their
   card in key-term groups and pinned terms instead.

   The Timeline tab works like Inquiries/Entities: once it has any rows,
   it fully replaces COURSE_DATA.timeline — the same shared timeline
   strip shown on every lesson, in whatever order the rows are in (so
   keep them chronological). Which part of it lights up/centers for a
   given lesson is controlled separately, per lesson — see "Timeline
   Focus" in the per-lesson tab section below.

   "title" is the short label shown directly on the timeline strip —
   keep it brief so the strip doesn't get cluttered. "details" is the
   longer description, only shown when a student clicks the small info
   circle on that entry — leave it blank if there's nothing more to say
   than the title itself (the info circle just won't show up for that
   entry). "category" is optional — a tag like "Suffrage," "Muckrakers,"
   or "World War I" to help students tell entries apart at a glance. Any
   category value you use gets its own color automatically (no need to
   assign colors yourself), consistently reused for every entry sharing
   that same category. If you have existing entries using an "event"
   column from before this changed, they still work exactly as before —
   "event" is used as both the title and the details until you update
   them to use the new columns instead.

   The timeline strip is a true year axis now, not just entries laid
   out in a row — each entry is positioned proportionally to its actual
   date, so entries close together in time sit close together, and
   entries decades apart end up decades apart. Every timeline strip
   also shows a row of president bands underneath it, one for every
   president you have set up — no setup beyond what you're probably
   already doing on the Entities tab. It looks for entities with role
   "President" whose actions include a year range like "1881-1885"
   anywhere in the text — "President from 1881-1885," "18th President,
   1881-1885," whatever phrasing you use is fine, as long as there's a
   year range somewhere in one of that entity's actions (dashes,
   en-dashes, or "to" as the separator all work). Each president's band
   spans the actual width of their term on that same year axis, and
   clicking a band opens that president's full entry. Bands covering
   any of the current lesson's highlighted ("Timeline Focus") years are
   highlighted, so it's easy to spot which presidencies are relevant to
   what you're looking at even with every president shown. If a
   president's actions don't have a year range anywhere, that one just
   doesn't get a band — not an error, just nothing to show for them.

   The Lessons tab is a bit different from the others: once it has ANY
   rows, it becomes the COMPLETE list of lessons for the whole site —
   not just an addition to data.js.
     - A row whose id matches a lesson already in data.js keeps that
       lesson's full content (readings, videos, questions, everything)
       and just applies the sheet's number/title.
     - A row with a new id creates a bare placeholder lesson — so you can
       sketch out lesson names before writing any real content.
     - Any lesson in data.js that ISN'T listed in the sheet will stop
       appearing on the site — even if it's fully written. Once you start
       using this tab, every lesson you want visible needs a row here.
     - If the sheet is empty or fails to load, nothing changes — data.js
       is used exactly as written.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   PER-LESSON TABS (LESSON_SHEETS, below)
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Separate from the four tabs above: give any lesson its OWN dedicated
   tab (e.g. a tab literally called "1-1 Growth of Cities") with these
   columns:

       Include | Inquiry | Inquiry ID | Objective | Mention Name | Mention | Key Term | Timeline Focus | Timeline Start Year | Timeline End Year |
       Reading Title | Reading Source | Reading Note | Reading Text | Reading Image | Reading Embed URL |
       Video Title | Video Length | Video Note | Video Src | Video Image |
       Image Title | Image Note | Image Files |
       Question Prompt | Question Type | Question Choices | Question Correct Index | Question Explanation | Question Sample Answer | Question Objectives | Question Image | Question Reading | Question After Paragraph | Question Video | Question Gallery | Do Now | Question Skill | Question Term Group | Question Group |
       Preset Label | Preset Label Color | Question Requires Approval |
       Key Term Group | Key Term Order | Key Term Pin | Source Pin |
       Reading Term Group | Video Term Group | Image Term Group |
       Reading Order | Video Order | Image Order | Set Order |
       Reading Set | Video Set | Image Set

   Each column is read independently, top to bottom, ignoring blanks —
   an objective in row 3 doesn't need anything in the Mention column on
   that same row. Put "yes" (or "true"/"y"/"1") in Key Term for any
   mention important enough to feature under "What You Need to Know" —
   leave it blank for a plain mention.

   Timeline highlighting works two ways — use whichever's easier. Note:
   the timeline strip itself is currently removed from the lesson page
   (it got too cluttered with a large number of entries, being
   reworked), so neither of these currently shows anything on the
   page — but they're harmless to keep filling in, and will pick back
   up automatically whenever the timeline display returns.

     "Timeline Focus": one id per row, typed directly (check your
     Timeline tab for the id of whichever event(s) this lesson covers,
     e.g. "t3", and list each one on its own row).

     "Timeline Start Year" / "Timeline End Year": simpler — just give a
     year (or a year range) and the site finds every entry in your
     Timeline tab whose Date falls in that range, automatically, no ids
     needed. A single year in Start Year with End Year left blank
     matches just that one year. This only kicks in if "Timeline Focus"
     is completely empty — if you've listed any ids there, those are
     used instead.

   Leaving all of these blank leaves the lesson's highlighted timeline
   events untouched in data.js. Keep in mind this can only highlight
   events that actually exist in your Timeline tab — if nothing there
   falls in the year range you give, nothing will highlight, and you'd
   need to add a row to the Timeline tab itself for that gap.

   Sources (readings, videos, images) work a bit differently — instead
   of one column, each is a GROUP of columns, and one full row (across
   just that group) = one reading, one video, or one image. Only a row
   with something in the Title column counts as a real entry, so you can
   mix reading rows, video rows, and image rows freely without them
   interfering with each other:

       Reading Title | Reading Source | Reading Note | Reading Text
       Video Title   | Video Length   | Video Note   | Video Src
       Image Title   | Image Note     | Image Files
       Image URL     | Gallery Name   | Image Caption   (alternative to Image Files — see below)

   Reading Text: separate paragraphs by typing three pipes — ||| — between
   them, all on one line (spaces around it are fine, they get trimmed).
   For example, typed directly into the cell:

       First paragraph about urbanization. ||| Second paragraph about tenements.

   That becomes two separate paragraphs on the site. Simpler and more
   reliable than trying to get a real line break into a spreadsheet cell.

   Each ||| -separated chunk can also be styled as something other than a
   plain paragraph — start it with one of these:

       # Text yields Heading 1
       ## Text yields Heading 2
       > Text yields a block quote

   For example: "# The Rise of Tenements ||| Regular paragraph text here. ||| > A quoted line from a primary source."
   becomes a heading, then a normal paragraph, then a block quote.

   Video Src: a file path, e.g. "videos/yourfile.mp4" — the file itself
   still needs to actually be uploaded into your videos/ folder. A YouTube
   or Vimeo link works too, and so does a GOOGLE DRIVE link: in Drive,
   right-click the video -> Share -> General access: "Anyone with the
   link" -> Copy link, and paste that link here. (It's shown in Drive's
   own player, so students need no Google sign-in only if it's shared
   that way. A Drive video also works in Reading Embed URL.)

   Reading Embed URL: optional — a URL to embed as a live, interactive
   page right inside that reading, e.g. a primary-source page on
   archive.org or the Library of Congress — or a YouTube link, which
   works here too (a regular youtube.com/watch or youtu.be link is
   fine, it gets converted the same way the Video Src column does; no
   need to find the special "embed" URL yourself). Shows above the
   reading's text, so it works alongside written content or entirely on
   its own if you leave Reading Text blank. Some sites block being
   embedded this way (their own security setting, nothing this site can
   work around) — if a reading's embed shows blank, that site doesn't
   allow it, and a plain link in Reading Text is the fallback.

   Each source in the list also shows a small circular icon, same style
   as the People/Topics/Events chips:
     - Reading Image / Video Image: optional — a URL to a small photo/
       thumbnail. Leave blank and that source just shows no icon.
     - Video: if left blank AND the video is a YouTube link, a thumbnail
       is pulled automatically — nothing to fill in for those.
     - Image sources always use their own first photo as the icon
       automatically — no separate column needed.

   Image Files: comma-separated paths, e.g. "images/a.jpg, images/b.jpg"
   — one path is a single image, two or more automatically becomes a
   gallery with Prev/Next. Same deal: the files need to really exist in
   your images/ folder.

   If putting multiple URLs in one cell feels cramped, there's a cleaner
   alternative: use "Image URL", "Gallery Name", and "Image Caption"
   instead of "Image Files" — one image per row, with a shared "Gallery
   Name" linking the rows that belong together, and an optional caption
   specific to that one image (shown under it when it's the one being
   viewed — different from "Image Note", which describes the gallery as
   a whole):

       Image Title           | Image Note      | Image URL         | Gallery Name          | Image Caption
       Tenement Photographs  | Sample gallery  | https://.../a.jpg | Tenement Photographs  | A family outside their tenement, 1890.
                              |                 | https://.../b.jpg | Tenement Photographs  | Interior of a crowded tenement room.
       Photo 2                | note 2         | https://.../c.jpg |                        |

   Rows sharing the same Gallery Name become one gallery, in the order
   they appear. A blank Gallery Name (like "Photo 2" above) makes that
   row its own standalone image. Only the first row in a group needs the
   Title/Note filled in — later rows just need the URL (and Caption, if
   you want one). Both styles ("Image Files" and "Image URL"/"Gallery
   Name") work at the same time, so use whichever feels clearer, or mix
   them.

   On the site, clicking any image opens it fullscreen (tap/click again
   or press Escape to exit) — no setup needed, that's automatic.

   Check Your Understanding: one row = one standalone question, only
   counted if Question Prompt has something in it.

       Question Type: "multiple_choice" (default if left blank) or
       "short_answer".

       Question Choices: comma or semicolon separated, multiple_choice only.

       Question Correct Index: 0-based position in Choices — 0 is the
       first choice, 1 is the second, etc. multiple_choice only.

       Question Sample Answer: short_answer only, revealed on tap.

       Question Objectives: comma-separated NUMBERS (not ids) referring to
       this same tab's Objective column by position — "1, 3" tags a
       question to the 1st and 3rd objective you listed. Optional.

       Question Image: optional, a URL or path to an image shown right
       under the prompt — a photo, map, or chart the question refers to.

       Question Reading / Question After Paragraph, OR Question Video, OR
       Question Gallery: leave all of these blank and the question shows
       up in "Check Your Understanding" at the end of the lesson, same as
       before. Fill in exactly ONE of the following to attach it
       somewhere specific instead:
         - Question Reading (+ Question After Paragraph) — drops it
           INSIDE a specific reading, right after a given paragraph.
           Question Reading must exactly match a title from this same
           tab's Reading Title column. Question After Paragraph: 0 =
           right after the reading's 1st paragraph, 1 = after the 2nd,
           and so on.
         - Question Video — shows it below that video once it's open.
           Must exactly match a title from this tab's Video Title column.
         - Question Gallery — shows it below that image or gallery once
           it's open. Must exactly match a title from this tab's Image
           Title (or Gallery Name) column.
       Questions can be mixed freely — some tied to a reading, video, or
       gallery, others left blank for the standalone list — all in the
       same tab, any row order.

       Do Now: check this box and the question ignores every other
       placement above — instead, it pops up automatically the moment
       this lesson opens, before the student sees anything else. Once a
       student's ID has answered it, it won't show again for them, even
       on a later visit — checked against their locally recorded answers.
       Any number of questions can have this checked for the same lesson
       — they're answered one after another in the order they appear in
       the tab, same as a Question Group (below), just without needing
       one. Insert a real checkbox via Insert -> Checkbox in Sheets, same
       as the Include column for Inquiries.

       Lesson Skills: (a column on a lesson tab) the skills EVERYONE practices in this
       lesson, e.g. Central Ideas; Cite Text Evidence (one per row or separated by ; ).
       Focus Skills: (a column on the Roster tab) skills a particular student works on,
       e.g. Using Context Clues. Together with Question Skill they decide who sees what:
         - a question with no Question Skill is shown to everyone
         - a question whose skill is in the lesson's Lesson Skills is shown to everyone
         - any other skilled question is shown ONLY to students who have that skill in
           their Focus Skills (so some students see questions others don't)
         - if a lesson has no Lesson Skills at all, everyone sees every question
       No reading, video or image shows a student more than 5 questions: if more apply,
       the student's own Focus Skill questions are kept first. (The teacher ID sees all.)

       Question Skill: a free-text tag for whatever category this
       question is really testing — "Cause and Effect," "Primary Source
       Analysis," "Chronological Thinking," whatever categories are
       useful to you. Unlike Question Objectives (which are specific to
       one lesson's own list), this is meant to be reused across lessons
       and across the whole year, so the same skill tag can show up on
       questions in totally different units. It's shown to students as a
       small tag on the question, and gets included in every answer sent
       to Teacher View, the live dashboard, and your spreadsheet — so you
       can filter or pivot by Skill later to see how students are doing
       on a given category over time, not just on a given lesson.

       Question Term Group: instead of (or alongside) a static image,
       shows every entity on your Entities tab whose role or type
       matches what you type here — e.g. typing "Muckraker" shows every
       person tagged with that role as a small photo-and-name chip above
       the question. Matches case-insensitively against the same
       role/type column entities use (see the Entities tab section
       above), and only entities with that exact role/type show up — if
       none match, nothing renders, so it's safe to leave blank on any
       question that doesn't need it. Works the same whether the
       question is a normal Check Your Understanding question or a Do
       Now.

       Question Group: give two or more questions at the exact same spot
       (same Question Reading + Question After Paragraph, same Question
       Video, same Question Gallery, or all checked Do Now) the same
       text here, and students answer them one after another as a
       sequence — the next one only appears once the current one's
       answered — instead of seeing every question at once. Any exact
       text works as long as it matches across the rows you want
       grouped ("Group A" is as good as anything more descriptive).
       Leave it blank and a question behaves exactly as it always has,
       on its own. Questions at different spots are never grouped
       together even if they happen to share the same group text.

       Preset Label: put a label name here (any row — it doesn't need a
       question, reading, video, or anything else on that row) and it
       shows up as a ready-made choice when a student on THIS lesson
       highlights a passage and saves it to their notes — right
       alongside any labels they've already made themselves, so they
       aren't stuck inventing categories from scratch. One label per
       row; use several rows to offer several presets on the same
       lesson (e.g. "Corruption", "Reform", "Labor" as three separate
       rows). If a student has already made a label with the same name
       on their own, this one takes over its color, so the same label
       stays visually consistent everywhere it's used. Leave this
       column blank everywhere and nothing changes — labels work
       exactly as they do today, entirely student-created.

       Preset Label Color: optional, goes with Preset Label. Type one
       of: brass, stamp, forest, focus, ink. Leave it blank and a color
       is assigned automatically (cycling through that same list in the
       order your preset rows appear), which is enough for most cases —
       only fill this in if you want a specific label to always be a
       specific color.

       Key-term groups (terms shown side by side in a resource's popup):
       1. On a key term's row (the row with its id in Mention), type a
          group name in "Key Term Group", e.g. Trusts. Several groups
          for one term go in one cell separated by semicolons. Every term
          with the same group name ends up in that group.
       2. Optional "Key Term Order": a number that orders the terms inside
          their group (lowest first). Blank ones follow in sheet order.
       3. On a reading's, video's or image's own row, type the group name
          in "Reading Term Group" / "Video Term Group" / "Image Term
          Group". That resource's popup then shows the group's terms side
          by side, each with a Pin button. Two groups: Trusts; Labor.
       A term group also shows on the lesson page itself, in "People,
       Topics & Events", as a collapsible box just like a resource set
       (its terms move into the box instead of the People/Topics/Events
       lists). These are separate from resource sets below (sets group
       the resources; term groups group the key terms).

       Worked example (one lesson's tab; only the columns that matter):
         Mention Name | Key Term | Key Term Group | Key Term Order | Reading Title    | Reading Term Group | Reading Set   | Reading Order | Set Order
         Carnegie     |   [x]    | Steel & Oil    | 2              |                  |                    |               |               |
         Rockefeller  |   [x]    | Steel & Oil    | 1              |                  |                    |               |               |
         Sherman Act  |   [x]    | Laws           |                |                  |                    |               |               |
                      |          |                |                | Gospel of Wealth | Steel & Oil        | Gilded Voices | 1             | 1
                      |          |                |                | Standard Oil     | Steel & Oil; Laws  | Gilded Voices | 2             |
       Result: "Gospel of Wealth" shows Rockefeller then Carnegie side by
       side; "Standard Oil" shows both groups; and the two readings sit
       together in one set called "Gilded Voices". (Type the term's NAME
       in Mention Name — the Mention column fills in the id by itself.)
       Pinning terms from the sheet: put "yes" in "Key Term Pin" on a
       term's row (the row with its id in Mention) and that term is pinned
       for every student on that lesson. It shows in the "Pinned terms"
       panel at the bottom of each reading / video / image popup, marked
       "Pinned by your teacher" (students can't unpin it), and gets a
       small thumbtack on its chip on the lesson page. Students can still
       pin other terms themselves; those appear below yours. (A plain
       "Pin" column header works too.)

       Pinning sources from the sheet: put "yes" in "Source Pin" on the
       same row as a reading, video or image (for a gallery built from
       several rows, any one of its rows will do). That source is then
       pinned for every student on that lesson: it shows in the Pinned
       panel at the bottom of every other popup, and tapping it there
       opens it right beside what they're reading. Students can also pin
       sources themselves -- hover a source on the page and press Pin (or
       use the thumbtack in its popup's title bar). A plain "Pin Source"
       column header works too.

       Key terms layout: "People, Topics & Events" has a List / Tabs
       toggle at the top of its box. Tabs shows one tab per key-term
       group, then People, Topics and Events. Each student's choice is
       remembered on their own device.

       Reading Set / Video Set / Image Set: any text. Resources on the
       same lesson that share a set name are grouped together under that
       name in the Sources list — as a collapsible box — instead of
       being listed one after another. The set sits where its first
       resource is. Leave it blank and a resource stays on its own.

       Order: type numbers to put resources and sets in the order you
       want. "Reading Order" / "Video Order" / "Image Order" number a
       resource; "Set Order" (on any row of a set) numbers the whole set.
       Loose resources and sets share one scale at the top level (lowest
       first), and inside a set its resources follow their own Order
       numbers. Anything with no number stays in sheet order, after the
       numbered ones. Leave every order column blank and nothing moves.

       Theme: an optional "Theme" column on the Units tab sets that unit's look
       (a theme id or name from SITE_THEMES at the bottom of this file), and an
       optional "Theme" column on the Lessons tab sets one lesson's look (it wins
       over its unit's). Blank = inherit from the unit, then the site default.
       Teacher View's Themes tab can override a unit's theme for everyone (handy
       for seasonal looks).

       Sensitive Lesson: a checkbox column on the Lessons tab (or the Units tab for a
       whole unit; "Lock Theme" still works as the same column). Checked = seasonal
       themes (Halloween, Winter, ...) are never used or offered for that lesson, so a
       heavy topic can't end up with something silly. Students can still pick any
       other theme. Nothing is ever locked to a single theme.

       Question Requires Approval: a checkbox on a SHORT-ANSWER question's
       row. When checked, a student's answer to it is held back from
       classmates until you approve it in Teacher View -> Answers (a "Hide
       from classmates" / "Approve" control sits on each short-answer row
       there). The student always sees their own answer right away, with a
       note that it's waiting on your approval. You can also hide any
       individual short answer from classmates at any time from that same
       screen, whether or not the question requires approval.

       Icons: give a Unit an image on the Units tab, and a Lesson one on
       the Lessons tab, by adding an "Icon" column holding an image URL.
       It shows in the lesson picker, the header, and beside the lesson
       title. Leave it blank and nothing changes.

   Example:

       Question Prompt                                  | Question Type   | Question Choices          | Question Correct Index | Question Objectives | Question Reading      | Question Video            | Question Gallery      | Question After Paragraph
       What does this photograph show?                  | multiple_choice | A tenement, A factory     | 0                       |                      |                        |                            | Tenement Photographs  |
       What point does this video make about growth?     | short_answer    |                            |                         | 1                    |                        | Growth of Cities Overview |                        |
       Which decade saw the fastest urban growth?        | multiple_choice | 1880s, 1900s, 1920s       | 1                       | 1                    |                        |                            |                        |
       Reflect on how immigration shaped city life.      | short_answer    |                            |                         | 2                    |                        |                            |                        |

   Since this makes for a wide tab, it's worth freezing the header row
   (View → Freeze → 1 row) so you can still tell which column is which
   as you scroll right.

   Each column is read independently, top to bottom, ignoring blanks —
   an objective in row 3 doesn't need anything in the Mention column on
   that same row. Put "yes" (or "true"/"y"/"1") in Key Term for any
   mention important enough to feature under "What You Need to Know" —
   leave it blank for a plain mention.

   "Mention Name" is there so you can type a person/topic's actual name
   instead of memorizing ids — put a formula in "Mention" to look up the
   real id automatically:

       Mention: =XLOOKUP(B2, Entities!B:B, Entities!A:A, "")

   (adjust the row number/columns to match your sheet). Only "Mention" is
   actually read by the site — "Mention Name" is just there for you to
   type into.

   For Inquiries: since there are only a handful of them, list all of
   them once with their id (copy this same block into every lesson tab)
   and a checkbox in "Include" — check the ones that apply to THIS lesson:

       Include | Inquiry                                                    | Inquiry ID
       [ ]     | What impacts do new technologies have on people's lives?   | inq1
       [x]     | How does racism and discrimination impact marginalized people? | inq2
       [ ]     | How do people use protest and resistance to create change? | inq3
       [ ]     | What impacts people's support for a war?                   | inq4
       [x]     | To what extent is the American Dream achievable?           | inq5

   Insert real checkboxes via Insert → Checkbox in Sheets. Only checked
   rows apply — "Inquiry" is just there so you can read which question
   you're checking; only "Include" and "Inquiry ID" are actually read.

   Publish that tab (File → Share → Publish to web → CSV) and add it to
   LESSON_SHEETS below, keyed by the lesson's id. You just need that
   tab's gid — open the tab in your browser and read the number after
   "gid=" in the address bar:

       const LESSON_SHEETS = {
         "1-1": sheetUrl(1706828899)
       };

   Only lessons listed in LESSON_SHEETS are affected, and only the
   columns that actually have data in them — an empty Mention column
   leaves that lesson's mentions/keyTerms untouched in data.js, and no
   checked Inquiry rows leaves inquiryFocus untouched too, so you can
   fill in just objectives now and everything else later without losing
   anything.
   ========================================================================= */

